WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.250
- Thanks to CuriosityStream

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:02.740
for keeping LegalEagle in the air.

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.610
Get 26% off CuriosityStream and Nebula

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.460
using the link in the description.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.970
Last week, President Trump
issued two executive orders

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:11.790
that will ban two major Chinese apps

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:12.890
from the American market.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:14.610
The orders state that, in 45 days,

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:15.810
Americans will be prohibited

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.100
from carrying out any
transactions with TikTok

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.590
and its parent company
and from the app, WeChat.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.590
This means US companies and individuals

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.490
will no longer be permitted to
advertise with the platforms,

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.420
offer them for download via app stores,

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.670
or enter into licensing
agreements with them.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.410
This would effectively stop Americans

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.640
from accessing TikTok and WeChat.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.330
The Trump orders came as
a shock to US consumers,

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.720
who are generally accustomed
to being able to use

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.410
any app that's offered on the app stores

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:41.820
run by Apple or Google.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.130
And complicating this narrative,

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.590
President Trump has not
always remained on topic

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:48.150
when discussing the reason for these bans,

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.540
which ostensibly is national security.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.920
President Trump also raised
eyebrows by suggesting

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.810
that if Microsoft were to purchase TikTok,

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.370
thereby saving it from the ban,

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.070
they ought to pay the US
Treasury some kind of fee.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.500
- Because I guess it's China, essentially,

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:02.796
but more than anything else,

00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:05.400
I said a very substantial
portion of that price

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.030
is gonna have to come into the Treasury

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:10.400
of the United States because
we're making it possible

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.700
for this deal to happen.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.104
- Wait. What?

00:01:13.104 --> 00:01:15.163
Can the president ban a Chinese company

00:01:15.163 --> 00:01:16.610
through executive order?

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.420
Can the president demand a cut
of the proceeds of that sale?

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:20.380
And what's going to happen

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.884
to your second favorite
video sharing platform?

00:01:22.884 --> 00:01:25.717
(dramatic music)

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.460
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.270
because the president wants
to ban TikTok, WeChat,

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.115
and Tencent and sell them to Microsoft

00:01:35.115 --> 00:01:38.440
and get a cut of the sale
like a two-bit gangster.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.610
2020 is really weird.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:42.500
So let's first start off by talking about

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.490
what is TikTok and WeChat.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.640
Now full disclosure, I'm
not on TikTok at the moment.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.750
I tried to use it once,

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.510
but I found it baffling and horrifying

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.840
so I'm obviously the
best person to explain

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.250
what TikTok is, which I'm now going to do.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:00.650
So TikTok is a social media
app owned by ByteDance,

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:02.200
a tech company based in Beijing.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.850
ByteDance runs a content platform in China

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.270
and other areas of the
world known as Toutiao,

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.160
which I'm probably
incredibly mispronouncing,

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.840
which translates to Headlines.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.740
Originally, Headlines was a
news recommendation engine,

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.520
and now it has evolved into
a content delivery platform

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.700
reminiscent of Facebook.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.410
ByteDance started TikTok
in 2017 when it purchased

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.720
and repurposed social
media startup, Musical.ly

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.550
and the two video sharing platforms merged

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.780
into a global application
that has gained popularity,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.420
particularly among teenagers

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.290
who seemed to ditch social media platforms

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.670
just as fast as adults figure
out what to do with them.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.970
Now I asked you on
Twitter, what TikTok was

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.839
and your answers were both
illuminating and hilarious.

00:02:39.839 --> 00:02:42.160
Joe Vincent said, "A way of communicating

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.080
through shared cultural metaphors."

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.270
Filip Gunnarsson said,
"The sound of clock makes."

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:49.670
A lot of you said it was
just Vine or Vine 2.0,

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:52.090
which, you know, I get that reference.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:53.376
Eric Garland said, "It's like Vine,

00:02:53.376 --> 00:02:55.360
except instead of just sending movies,

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.610
it also sends your
keystrokes on other apps

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.920
to the same regime
committing economic espionage

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.270
every single day."

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:03.910
Bushra said, "A song by Kesha."

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:05.210
And some weirdo named

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:10.057
Hank Green: A Beautifully
Foolish Endeavor is Out! says,

00:03:10.057 --> 00:03:12.280
"It's a creation engine,
providing all the tools,

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:13.887
inspiration, and gratification necessary

00:03:13.887 --> 00:03:15.560
all in the same place."

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.650
Now, as you probably
know, TikTok's popularity

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.230
surged during the pandemic.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.900
The app was downloaded
over 623 million times

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.730
during the first six months of
2020, and its biggest markets

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:26.770
were India, Brazil, and the United States,

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:29.760
although it has received a
lot of scrutiny in India.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.190
Which takes us to WeChat,
another one of the companies

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.320
that's facing the executive orders.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.020
WeChat is the most used
messaging system in the world,

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:39.970
with more than a billion users.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:42.560
The app is owned by the
Chinese company, Tencent,

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.048
which is a major player in
the US video games industry.

00:03:45.048 --> 00:03:49.030
In China, almost 99% of
smartphone users use the app.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:49.880
WeChat is integrated

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.620
with just about every
aspect of a person's life,

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.828
from paying for dinner,
applying for a loan,

00:03:53.828 --> 00:03:56.730
watching a video, to ordering a rideshare.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.310
Chinese users can do all of that

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:00.690
without ever exiting the WeChat app.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.090
And people in the US and other
countries also use WeChat

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.340
to keep in touch with
friends and family in China.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.290
But WeChat has been linked
with Chinese state surveillance

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:11.041
and censorship, and odds are
that if you send a message

00:04:11.041 --> 00:04:13.210
to someone using WeChat in China

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:14.840
that's critical of the Chinese government,

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.680
then WeChat will block that.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.360
Which takes us to the
social data mining aspects

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.036
of both TikTok and WeChat.

00:04:21.036 --> 00:04:24.500
TikTok videos are obviously fun, if inane,

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.820
but there's also a dark
side to TikTok as well

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.670
and the answer lies in the app's ability

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:30.450
to collect information about its users.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:31.780
When you download TikTok,

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:33.720
you agree to its data collection policies.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.070
In addition to information
you choose to provide,

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:37.610
like your identity and billing info,

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.660
the app automatically collects other data.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.520
For example, if you choose to sign up

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:42.840
with your social media,

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.672
Instagram, Facebook,
Twitter, or Google accounts,

00:04:45.672 --> 00:04:47.850
TikTok reserves the right
to harvest information

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:51.300
from those services,
including your contact list

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.410
for these services and
information relating

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.570
to your use of the platform
in relation to those services.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:58.830
And then TikTok also collects
information about you

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:01.700
from third party services
like advertising partners.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.510
And then there are provisions like these,

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:05.365
quote, "Other Users of the Platform.

00:05:05.365 --> 00:05:07.470
Sometimes other users of the Platform

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.060
may provide us information about you,

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.103
including through customer
service inquiries.

00:05:11.103 --> 00:05:12.420
Other Sources.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:13.970
We may collect information about you

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.000
from other publicly available sources."

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.620
And when it comes to TikTok and WeChat,

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:19.940
the US and other nations are concerned

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.130
about the company's relationship
with the Chinese government

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.460
and what information TikTok may share.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:25.370
Although the United States

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:27.020
and other countries trade with China

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:29.280
and corporations do big
business in the country,

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.910
the Chinese government is still commanded

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:32.750
by the Chinese Communist Party

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:34.430
and it's not a free society.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.140
Chinese citizens don't
have freedom of speech

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:38.190
and the government controls
the flow of information.

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:40.210
China's 2017 National Intelligence Law

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:41.430
requires Chinese citizens

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:43.758
to support the government's
national security activities,

00:05:43.758 --> 00:05:45.860
including possibly aiding the country

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:48.180
in seeking intelligence
from foreign countries

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:49.280
and their citizens.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:50.700
TikTok's parent company, ByteDance,

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:52.860
has worked with the
Chinese Communist Party

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.650
to censor and surveil content

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:56.850
that the government did
not want widely known.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:58.390
For example, the government crackdown

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:01.040
on any mention of China's
repression of the Uyghurs,

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:05.300
a Turkic-speaking ethnic minority
living mostly in Xinjiang,

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.130
which I'm probably mispronouncing.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:09.250
The Uyghurs are Muslims from central Asia

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:11.480
who are supposed to have
significant autonomy

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.240
in the Xinjiang Province.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.550
However, the Chinese government
is arbitrarily arresting

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:17.960
and imprisoning Uyghurs,
censoring their language,

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.810
and even erasing social media posts

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:21.057
that mention the Uyghurs by name.

00:06:21.057 --> 00:06:25.057
(speaking in foreign language)

00:06:27.695 --> 00:06:30.160
- And ByteDance has helped the
government censor information

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:33.510
about the Uyghurs in its
Headlines platform and on TikTok.

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:36.510
Users have reported being
fed nothing but propaganda

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:39.310
when attempting to outwit
the app's algorithm

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:40.450
about the subject.

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:42.220
And naturally, this led to concerns

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.150
the company would be willing to share data

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:46.770
about its foreign users
with the Chinese government.

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:48.930
The US Army and Navy
banned service members

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.470
from downloading the app to
government-issued phones.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.390
The US House of
Representatives voted to bar

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:55.940
the use of TikTok on all
government-issued phones.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:58.820
And this isn't necessarily a
politically partisan issue.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:03.060
The House vote was 336 in
favor to just 71 opposed.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.750
And Chuck Schumer tweeted
his general agreement

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:08.620
with President Trump that
TikTok may hand over data

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:09.950
to the Chinese government.

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:10.820
And Secretary of State

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:12.780
Mike Pompeo said-
- We've urged countries

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:14.320
to become clean countries

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.670
so that their citizens'
private information

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:19.420
doesn't end up in the hands of
the Chinese Communist Party.

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:21.690
- And that the app created an opportunity

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:24.760
for the Chinese government
to spy on foreign nationals.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:26.940
Though just because the app
has banned on government phones

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:28.610
isn't really dispositive of the issue.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:31.320
Service members are also
prohibited from using Fitbits

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.676
after fitness data was used to identify

00:07:33.676 --> 00:07:35.920
different military installations

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:38.120
because of the GPS tracking.

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:40.350
But TikTok and ByteDance
have repeatedly denied

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:43.040
ever giving user data to
the Chinese Communist Party.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.250
The company claims that all
of its US data is stored

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:47.020
in data centers in Singapore,

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:49.260
not under the control of Chinese law.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:51.660
And Kevin Mayer, CEO of TikTok America,

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:53.360
also posted a statement promising

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:55.620
to make its data collection
process transparent.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.500
TikTok also said that the
company has tried to engage

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.130
with the US government for over a year,

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:02.515
but that the government
paid no attention to facts.

00:08:02.515 --> 00:08:05.240
Although ByteDance has
started talks with Microsoft

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:08.150
about simply selling its US
platform to a tech giant,

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:10.750
the company also vowed to
challenge the executive orders

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:11.730
in the courts.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:14.170
And we'll get to the executive
orders in just a moment,

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:15.780
but it's probably worthwhile to point out

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.080
that there may be significant

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:18.710
First Amendment issues here too

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:20.810
because it's a widely known secret

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:23.700
that President Trump
really hates both TikTok

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.893
and the content that's on TikTok.

00:08:25.893 --> 00:08:29.188
TikTok is a bastion of
satire of President Trump.

00:08:29.188 --> 00:08:31.692
People like Sarah Cooper,
who ridiculed President Trump

00:08:31.692 --> 00:08:35.720
by simply repeating what
he has said verbatim.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:38.500
- You know, TikTok, we
may be banning TikTok.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.580
We may be doing some other things.

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:41.830
There are a couple of options.

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:45.050
- And let's not forget about
all the K-pop stans on TikTok

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:47.540
who signed up for President
Trump's Tulsa rally.

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:51.170
- I got two tickets, but I totally forgot

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:55.260
that I have to pick every
individual piece of lint

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:56.960
off of my room floor.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.020
- And made it appear that
hundreds of thousands of people

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:01.150
were going to show up to
the rally when, really,

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:02.780
only a handful of people did,

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:04.490
which may have led to the ouster

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:07.220
of Trump's campaign
manager, Brad Parscale.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:08.830
So it's an open question as to whether

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:11.570
First Amendment speech
concerns are one of the reasons

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.414
why President Trump wants to
ban these particular apps.

00:09:14.414 --> 00:09:16.460
And certainly, eyebrows are raised

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:19.000
when the President bans
particular companies

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:21.600
as opposed to the practice of data sharing

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.820
that is the thing that is
ostensibly objectionable here.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:26.400
But let's talk about the executive orders

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.440
that are at issue here that
effectively ban TikTok,

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:29.633
Tencent, and WeChat.

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:33.040
President Trump's executive
orders effectively ban the use

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:34.520
of these apps in the United States.

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:37.210
And to do so, these executive
orders invoke the authority

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:40.330
under the International
Emergency Economic Powers Act,

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:43.680
or IEEPA, and state that
additional steps must be taken

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.810
to deal with the, quote,
"national emergency"

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.320
posed by these apps.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.780
Traditionally, when
presidents used the IEEPA Act

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:51.090
for national security purposes,

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.420
they rely on traditional
issues of national security,

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:56.630
such as access to classified
information on weapons

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:58.210
or intelligence systems.

00:09:58.210 --> 00:09:59.890
But President Trump's orders differ

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:02.400
in that they're primarily
targeting companies

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.960
because of data security
and data privacy policies.

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:08.700
So what do these executive orders say?

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:11.707
Well let's start with the TikTok
order, which states quote,

00:10:11.707 --> 00:10:14.490
"TikTok automatically captures
vast swaths of information

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:16.040
from its users, including internet

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:18.120
and other network activity information

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.016
such as location data and
browsing and search histories.

00:10:21.016 --> 00:10:22.830
The data collection threatens to allow

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:25.102
the Chinese Communist Party
access to Americans' personal

00:10:25.102 --> 00:10:28.220
and proprietary information,
potentially allowing China

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:31.540
to track the locations of federal
employees and contractors,

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:33.250
build dossiers of personal information

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:35.761
for blackmail and conduct
corporate espionage."

00:10:35.761 --> 00:10:37.810
As evidenced, the TikTok order says

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:39.260
the company censors content

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:41.377
in line with Chinese
Communist Party guidelines.

00:10:41.377 --> 00:10:43.790
The order also says the app could be used

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:45.160
for disinformation campaigns

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:47.240
that benefit the Chinese Communist Party.

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:48.117
The order says that, quote,

00:10:48.117 --> 00:10:49.897
"any transaction by any person

00:10:49.897 --> 00:10:52.750
with ByteDance or its
subsidiaries, TikTok,

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:54.900
will be prohibited in 45 days."

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:56.520
And this probably means Apple and Google

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.470
will no longer be able to list the app

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:00.140
in their respective app stores.

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:02.210
Which takes us to the
other executive order

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:03.320
regarding WeChat.

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:05.850
President Trump's order
on WeChat cited concern

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:08.780
that the app could be used to
spy on Chinese nationalists

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:11.110
who visit or immigrate
to the United States.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:14.110
The executive order states
that the app allows, quote,

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:15.980
the Chinese Communist Party a mechanism

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.564
for keeping tabs on Chinese citizens

00:11:17.564 --> 00:11:20.210
who may be enjoying the
benefits of a free society

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:21.640
for the first time in their lives."

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.030
The order also hits WeChat

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:25.850
for censoring the same
kind of content as TikTok.

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:28.273
Now the Chinese company,
Tencent, owns WeChat,

00:11:28.273 --> 00:11:30.790
but unlike the executive order on TikTok,

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:33.340
which extends to the
parent company, ByteDance,

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:37.600
the order appears not to apply
to subsidiaries of Tencent.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:39.700
A White House official told
the "Los Angeles Times"

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:43.160
that the band does not apply
to Tencent's subsidiaries

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:45.110
like Riot Games, which is the developer

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:46.240
of the League of Legends game,

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:47.880
which is very popular on the internet.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:50.900
And Tencent also has a
big stake in Epic Games,

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:54.060
which makes Fortnite, and
the government is clarifying

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:57.240
that those holdings are
not affected by this ban.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.760
But needless to say, if this
order extends to Tencent,

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:01.150
then it would be a major disruption

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:03.130
for the video game industry, but for now,

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:05.790
that crisis appears to have been averted.

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:07.750
But if you came here for the video games,

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:09.910
I'd highly recommend checking
out Richard Hoeg's channel,

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:12.490
who goes into depth about
these executive orders

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:14.370
and their effect on the
video game industry.

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:16.570
I'll put a link to that
down in the description.

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:19.300
But here, both orders
lack specific details.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:21.560
Both delegate authority
to the commerce secretary

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:24.200
to specify precisely what
transactions are banned

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:25.950
and which might still be allowed.

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.430
This means that we have to wait and see

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:29.810
what else the government
says about the orders

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:30.750
in the coming weeks.

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:32.190
So the orders may be broader

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:34.850
or narrower than we think
they are at the moment.

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:36.230
That'll have to be clarified.

00:12:36.230 --> 00:12:37.810
But let's talk about the
president's authority

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:39.840
to ban TikTok and WeChat.

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.100
TikTok teens are pretty
upset about the government's

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:44.530
potentially taking away a
platform that they love.

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:45.880
So the question is, does the government

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:47.630
have the authority to do so?

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:50.370
Well, the answer is, generally, yes.

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:51.620
And the discussion of those powers

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:54.010
starts with the IEEPA.

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:55.440
President Trump used his powers

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:57.920
under the International
Emergency Economic Power Act

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:00.060
to stop TikTok inside the US.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:02.500
Now the IEEPA was passed in 1977

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.300
actually to restrict
the president's powers

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.130
to declare an indefinite
emergency during peacetime.

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:08.970
But as with a lot of legislation

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:10.730
that was intended to be a restriction,

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:12.250
it can sometimes be flipped on its head

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:14.940
and allow the executive broad powers.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:17.460
The law here allows a president
to take extraordinary action

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:20.000
without consent of Congress,
but it requires the president

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:21.960
to declare a national security emergency

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:23.720
under the National Emergencies Act.

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:25.530
And the IEEPA allows the president

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.480
to declare a national emergency

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:31.150
to, quote, "deal with any
unusual or extraordinary threat,

00:13:31.150 --> 00:13:33.564
which has its source
outside of the United States

00:13:33.564 --> 00:13:35.910
to the national security, foreign policy,

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:37.910
or economy of the United States."

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:40.610
Now those are obviously
extremely broad powers

00:13:40.610 --> 00:13:42.070
that give the president discretion

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:44.540
to investigate, block, regulate, compel,

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:46.930
or prohibit the importation, transfer,

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:49.820
or acquisition of property
in which any foreign country

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:52.450
or national thereof has any interest."

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:54.210
The IEEPA's powers allow the president

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:55.800
to act with respect to any person,

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:58.025
quote, "subject to the
jurisdiction of the United States."

00:13:58.025 --> 00:14:00.050
And to exercise these broad powers,

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:02.036
the president merely has to
make an initial declaration

00:14:02.036 --> 00:14:04.330
of a national security and justify

00:14:04.330 --> 00:14:05.960
that declaration to Congress.

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:07.430
And theoretically, initially,

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:09.570
the president had an
ongoing responsibility

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:12.470
to consult with Congress, at
least once every six months,

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:15.080
in order to continue
the national emergency.

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:16.750
And the IEEPA originally gave Congress

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:19.250
the power to stop the president
from using these powers

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:21.090
if, through a concurrent resolution,

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:23.430
Congress declares that
the emergency is over.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:25.790
However, the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:28.500
held that this provision
was unconstitutional.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:30.420
Now, if Congress wants to terminate

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:32.780
the president's authority
under an IEEPA decision,

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:35.310
legal experts presume that
it would require passing

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:37.360
a veto-proof joint resolution

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:39.220
terminating the national emergency,

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:42.360
and no such resolution
has ever passed Congress.

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:43.970
And here, the executive orders rely

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:46.609
on a national emergency
declaration made over a year ago

00:14:46.609 --> 00:14:51.609
in May of 2019, specifically
Executive Order 13873.

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:53.560
That emergency declaration emphasized

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:56.020
the threat associated with
foreign owned communication

00:14:56.020 --> 00:14:58.980
and information technology
companies and services operating

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:00.610
in the United States in circumstances

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:03.150
where the foreign company
is subject to the authority

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:05.520
of a, quote, "foreign adversary."

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:07.130
So in terms of whether it's legal,

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:09.130
the answer is, probably, yes.

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:11.369
As we've seen in a number
of different circumstances,

00:15:11.369 --> 00:15:14.900
what we have is broad
delegated powers from Congress,

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:16.780
arguably intended for situations

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:18.900
other than what they're
being used for here,

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:21.130
but Congress hasn't revoked that power.

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:25.050
And when the executive has broad
authority, as it does here,

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:27.680
it's apt to use that broad authority.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:30.123
Congress has the ability to
revoke that authorization,

00:15:30.123 --> 00:15:31.540
but number one, it doesn't appear

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:33.187
that Congress has the appetite to do so,

00:15:33.187 --> 00:15:35.941
and number two, it would
need to pass legislation

00:15:35.941 --> 00:15:39.240
that would withstand a presidential veto,

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:40.982
which would be a very tall order.

00:15:40.982 --> 00:15:44.150
So you can see that Congress
delegated wide latitude

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:46.160
to the president to set policy

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:48.060
using the powers delegated in IEEPA.

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:50.790
And while Congress granted
sweeping powers to the president,

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:53.610
at least Congress did put
some limits on those powers.

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:56.270
So let's talk about some
of those exceptions.

00:15:56.270 --> 00:15:57.770
There are exceptions to IEEPA

00:15:57.770 --> 00:16:00.350
that might apply to the
president's executive orders.

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:03.960
IEEPA is located at 50 USC Section 1702,

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:07.060
and Subsection B of 1702 states exceptions

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:08.160
to the grant of authority.

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:09.870
And specifically it
says that the authority

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:11.510
granted to the president by this section

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:13.840
does not include the authority to regulate

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:16.570
or prohibit directly or indirectly, one,

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:19.070
any postal, telegraphic, telephonic,

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:20.470
or other personal communication,

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:23.050
which does not involve the
transfer of anything of value.

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:24.370
And then there's Subsection 3

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:26.210
that creates an exception
for the importation,

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:28.818
from any country or the
exportation to any country,

00:16:28.818 --> 00:16:30.810
whether commercial or otherwise,

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:33.010
regardless of format or
medium of transmission,

00:16:33.010 --> 00:16:36.010
of any information or
informational materials,

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:39.154
including but not limited to,
publications, films, posters,

00:16:39.154 --> 00:16:42.100
other forms of media ending with a list

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:44.250
that ends with newswire feeds.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:46.010
So obviously, TikTok is going to argue

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:48.160
that these exceptions
are directly on point

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:51.550
and prohibit the president
from banning their company

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:52.620
and their practices.

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:54.830
In the first instance,
the first subsection deals

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:58.180
with personal communications,
which TikTok is nothing

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:01.280
if not a way for people to
deliver personal communications

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:04.950
in the form of short videos
to individuals and groups.

00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:07.290
And then the third subsection deals

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:09.370
with information transmission,

00:17:09.370 --> 00:17:12.440
the newswires and informational materials.

00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:14.160
This may apply to the platform itself,

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:17.170
but it also may apply to
the practices of TikTok

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:20.940
of taking the information
of its users and customers

00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:25.060
and transmitting it to,
potentially, other places.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:27.520
So it's possible that
these exceptions may apply.

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:30.690
And if I were TikTok, I
would go before the courts

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:33.020
and I would seek a
temporary restraining order

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:34.840
and, eventually, a permanent injunction

00:17:34.840 --> 00:17:36.840
preventing the president's
executive orders

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:38.120
from going into effect.

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:40.700
So now the question is,
can TikTok do anything

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:42.210
to stop these orders?

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:43.620
Does TikTok have any power

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:45.920
to ask for judicial review
of the president's decision?

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:47.430
And the answer there is yes.

00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:49.300
The president's declarations under IEEPA

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:51.840
are not specifically exempt
from judicial review.

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.450
But keep in mind that the IEEPA
relates to foreign affairs,

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:55.940
and this is an area where the president

00:17:55.940 --> 00:17:58.700
has greater discretion
to act without Congress.

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:00.850
The president's authority
over foreign affairs is rooted

00:18:00.850 --> 00:18:02.240
in Article II of the Constitution,

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:03.530
which gives the president the ability

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:05.650
to make treaties and appoint ambassadors.

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.100
Other clauses also make the
president the commander in chief

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:11.160
of the Army and Navy, with a
whole host of implied powers,

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:13.240
like the authority to
recognize foreign governments

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:15.550
and conduct diplomacy
with other countries.

00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:17.880
And as commander in chief,
the president has many powers

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:19.920
involving the collection
of foreign intelligence

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:21.540
and use of military power.

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:23.750
It would definitely not
be an easy case to win.

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:25.500
The Supreme Court has held that IEEPA,

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:27.610
read in conjunction with the
Trading with the Enemy Act,

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:30.120
or TWEA, gives the
president wide discretion

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:33.040
to impose embargoes on products
from foreign countries.

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:34.110
The Supreme Court determined

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:35.920
that the IEEPA was lawfully employed

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:39.510
to impose restrictions on travel
into and trading with Cuba.

00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:41.950
Lower federal courts have
also upheld the IEEPA

00:18:41.950 --> 00:18:43.510
against First Amendment free speech

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:45.610
and Fifth Amendment
due process challenges.

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:48.570
And it, in fact, appears
that TikTok is filing suit.

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:50.960
NPR is reporting that
TikTok is filing suit

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:52.320
against the Trump administration,

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:54.804
arguing that the US government
conducted no investigation

00:18:54.804 --> 00:18:57.338
into the company before levying this ban,

00:18:57.338 --> 00:19:00.060
and which would presumably constitute

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:01.700
some sort of due process violation

00:19:01.700 --> 00:19:04.020
because they were not
given any kind of rights

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:06.420
before their company
was effectively banned

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:07.920
by executive fiat.

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:10.600
And TikTok is arguing that
it's being singled out,

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:14.010
which seems probably reasonable
under the circumstances.

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:16.010
It seems that TikTok and WeChat

00:19:16.010 --> 00:19:17.600
are directly being singled out

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:19.720
as opposed to other potential companies

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:23.820
who are conducting similar
information-gathering practices.

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:25.290
And I'm sure that they're going to argue

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:27.440
that the exceptions to IEEPA apply

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:29.500
and that they fall under the exceptions,

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:31.560
and thus, the president
doesn't even have the power

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:33.290
to issue the executive orders

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:35.550
which purport to ban TikTok.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:38.060
As that lawsuit is filed and litigated,

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:39.660
we'll see how that turns out.

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:42.070
TikTok's best shot at
overturning the decision

00:19:42.070 --> 00:19:43.970
might be challenging it as an attack

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:46.010
on the First Amendment
right of freedom of speech.

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:47.130
Now the complicating factor

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:49.250
is that the government
has gotten way more leeway

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:51.210
to restrict speech since Congress enacted

00:19:51.210 --> 00:19:53.310
the US Patriot Act after September 11th.

00:19:53.310 --> 00:19:55.420
And many lawsuits involving the IEEPA

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:57.860
and the Trading with the
Enemy Act are also in context

00:19:57.860 --> 00:20:00.240
with groups associated
with funding terrorism.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:02.228
And if TikTok makes a
First Amendment case,

00:20:02.228 --> 00:20:03.520
it would need to claim

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:05.840
that the executive order
is not content neutral;

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:07.510
in other words, that the
company would need to claim

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:09.290
that, in order to explicitly discriminate

00:20:09.290 --> 00:20:12.430
only against certain types of
speech or certain speakers.

00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:14.610
But even that would be an
incredibly uphill battle

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:16.360
because the government would just point

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:18.930
to data collection practices
and sharing practices,

00:20:18.930 --> 00:20:21.080
which are likely to be content neutral

00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:23.640
in the sense that all of
the data is either shared

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:25.270
or has the possibility of being shared

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:26.820
with the Chinese government.

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:28.010
And the US government would claim

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:30.420
that that gives it a
basis to restrict the apps

00:20:30.420 --> 00:20:34.650
and that it's not related
to perceived anti-Trump bias

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:36.927
that is contained in the platforms.

00:20:36.927 --> 00:20:39.360
So while there are challenges available,

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:42.470
it seems like the Trump
administration has the ability

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:44.350
to ban these apps.

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:46.606
So the question is, would
a sale of these apps

00:20:46.606 --> 00:20:49.620
save them and allow them
to continue to operate?

00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:51.580
Before these executive
orders were handed down,

00:20:51.580 --> 00:20:53.520
Microsoft indicated that
it would be interested

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:56.420
in acquiring aspects
of TikTok's operations

00:20:56.420 --> 00:20:58.930
and allow the app to still be
available to American users.

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:00.680
But it isn't clear whether this deal

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:01.940
could even work at the moment.

00:21:01.940 --> 00:21:03.500
Microsoft would only be able to buy

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:04.670
a regional portion of the company,

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:06.950
that part which operates
in the United States.

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:09.750
And the orders would seem to
prohibit Microsoft's TikTok

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:11.387
from having ongoing legal relationships

00:21:11.387 --> 00:21:14.150
with TikTok operations
still owned by ByteDance

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:15.550
operating in other countries.

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:17.100
It could also impact licensing deals

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:18.660
for people who make money on TikTok,

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:21.180
who assume that their content
is going to be available

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:24.230
on the other platforms that
are owned by ByteDance.

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:27.010
And the 45-day timeframe
here is very short.

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:30.150
If WeChat or TikTok were
to be saved by a sale,

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:32.250
the transactions would be very complex.

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:34.730
Deals like that will be
done within six weeks,

00:21:34.730 --> 00:21:36.540
although the commerce secretary presumably

00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:37.890
could extend that deadline.

00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:40.780
And here, it looks like
TikTok will probably be sold.

00:21:40.780 --> 00:21:42.400
It's an external subsidiary,

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:45.580
so the American operations
are fairly bifurcated

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:48.490
from those operations in China
and the rest of the world.

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:50.500
So it's possible to separate it

00:21:50.500 --> 00:21:53.020
and sell it cleanly to
an American company.

00:21:53.020 --> 00:21:55.030
But WeChat and Tencent are different.

00:21:55.030 --> 00:21:57.270
Those companies primarily operate in China

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:58.990
and there doesn't appear
to be any clear way

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:03.360
to separate their US operations
from the Chinese operations.

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:06.000
And in addition, even if
these companies are sold

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.440
to American companies
and continue to operate,

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:11.020
we should all be worried about retaliation

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:13.520
by the Chinese government
and Chinese companies.

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:15.210
China has a much longer history

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:17.490
of nationalizing
companies than the US does

00:22:17.490 --> 00:22:19.040
and it's entirely possible

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:21.150
that China is not going
to take this sitting down.

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:23.530
It's possible that China may force

00:22:23.530 --> 00:22:25.530
American companies operating in China

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:27.720
to divest from their holdings in China

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:30.960
and sell those holdings
to a Chinese company.

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:32.950
It's possible, as some have speculated,

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:35.570
that China might
retaliate by forcing Apple

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:38.600
to give up all of its
manufacturing arms in China

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:40.351
and sell them to a Chinese company.

00:22:40.351 --> 00:22:42.960
We don't know what the
unintended consequences

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:45.100
of these actions are going to be.

00:22:45.100 --> 00:22:46.820
And perhaps the strangest legal question

00:22:46.820 --> 00:22:48.482
raised by all of these different issues

00:22:48.482 --> 00:22:50.620
is whether President Trump can demand

00:22:50.620 --> 00:22:52.520
what is effectively a VIG.

00:22:52.520 --> 00:22:54.030
What about President Trump's suggestion

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:55.340
that some portion of a sale

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:57.690
could be given to the US Treasury?

00:22:57.690 --> 00:22:59.470
- Really the Treasury,
I guess you would say,

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:02.080
of the United States gets a lot of money.

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:04.330
- Yeah, here it appears
that President Trump

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:06.640
was just spitballing like a mob boss,

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:09.890
asking for a VIG from a
local mom and pop bodega.

00:23:09.890 --> 00:23:11.490
There doesn't appear to be any law

00:23:11.490 --> 00:23:12.470
that would allow the government

00:23:12.470 --> 00:23:15.150
to take a percentage of the
funds from the sale of TikTok,

00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:17.340
you know, other than, you know, taxes.

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:20.070
That give me a cut mentality of a sale

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:22.193
is literally the kind of thing
that can get a politician

00:23:22.193 --> 00:23:24.785
in trouble for commercial bribery.

00:23:24.785 --> 00:23:26.170
And that's actually what happened

00:23:26.170 --> 00:23:27.754
to Illinois Governor Rod Blagojevich,

00:23:27.754 --> 00:23:30.170
who President Trump
pardoned earlier this year.

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:31.780
Now granted, that was in the context

00:23:31.780 --> 00:23:33.450
of personal gain for the governor,

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:35.600
not funds going to the Treasury,

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:40.590
but also, any kinds of funds
as a sort of key man cut

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:42.210
from the sale would be so small

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:43.700
in the context of the American government

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:47.160
that one wonders why you would even bother

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:48.070
with such a thing.

00:23:48.070 --> 00:23:49.940
But since there's no
evidence or legal ability

00:23:49.940 --> 00:23:51.280
for the president to take a percentage

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:53.480
of any sale for himself or the Treasury,

00:23:53.480 --> 00:23:54.570
that's probably not something

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:56.820
that we have to worry about for now.

00:23:56.820 --> 00:23:57.880
For now.

